    description:"Copy a Session phrase or audio clip to an existing compatible track, preserving the source and destination processing.",
    arguments:[{name:"request",description:"Source part, destination track/slot, optional copy name and audition intent.",required:true}],
    build:({request})=>({description:"Reuse one Session clip on another track.",messages:[userMessage(`Reuse this Session part: ${request}
1. Read get_session_orientation, get_selection_context, list_tracks and get_transport_recording_context. Resolve the explicit source and destination before relying on selection. Detail clip and highlighted slot can differ. Resolve exact Session clip IDs through list_clips on both tracks. Ask one focused question only when materially different sources or destinations remain; duplicate names alone do not establish identity. This copies a clip, not an instrument, track, scene or Arrangement passage.
2. Read complete source MIDI notes and get_clip_timing, or get_audio_clip_state and timing for audio. Inspect the existing destination devices and mixer/routing as needed to explain its role. MIDI must go to an ordinary MIDI track and audio to an ordinary audio track. Choose an existing empty destination slot. Never overwrite content, create tracks/scenes, load an instrument, change routing or convert MIDI/audio as a shortcut. Those actions need their own requested scope. If no suitable slot exists, resolve the destination with the user. Keep both source and destination processing unchanged.
3. Explain the musical consequence. MIDI notes will use the destination's existing instrument and processing; copying them does not copy the source sound or guarantee audible output. Audio copies share their source file and use the destination processing; this is not a rendered stem or independent sample backup. Envelope contents, their cross-track retargeting and hidden expression are not verified. Do not promise the copied automation will control equivalent devices. Resolve any automation-dependent request before treating the copy as an equivalent performance.
4. Plan duplicate_clip with trackId and clipId for the source, destinationTrackId, exact destinationClipId, optional descriptive name and fresh expectedStateVersion. Omitting destinationTrackId keeps the existing same-track behavior. The updated bridge checks both native owners/slots, source clip identity and complete exposed content, destination compatibility and emptiness, freeze/recording state and pending source/destination launches. Limits are 4096 stored notes or warp markers. Read the exact source/destination names and contents in the plan, then confirm the unchanged plan. Do not stop recording, unfreeze a track or alter a performance to bypass a refusal without authority.
5. Require copyVerified, the expected sourceTrackId/sourceClipId and copiedClipId. The returned trackId and clips list belong to the destination. Independently read the copy's complete notes/timing or audio state; new note IDs can differ. Re-read the source and destination device/mixer baselines to check preservation. Verification covers exposed clip state, not all envelope points, hidden state or file bytes. If native copying throws before ownership of a new clip is established, partial content is retained. Cleanup removes only a confirmed new copy; uncertainty or cleanup failure stays explicit. After a lost reply inspect both exact slots and their material before any retry; never replay the copy or delete ambiguous content.
6. Audition only when requested or already authorized. Read destination recording/launch context, then launch the exact copied clip with an observed one-shot quantization. Launch can start global playback, replace that track's Session playback and override Arrangement playback; other tracks may sound. Preserve unrelated mutes, solos, arms, monitoring, routing and devices. A muted track or a copy receipt does not prove the user heard it. Ask what the user hears before refining. Keep the source and chosen alternative; further note edits, sound changes, deletion and saving need their own scope. Avoid global undo and report the exact source, destination, observed differences and any partial state. ${confirmWorkflow}`)]})
