    build:({request})=>({description:"Prepare and verify a native WAV mixdown.",messages:[userMessage(`Export this mix: ${request}
1. Read get_live_state, get_session_orientation, get_song_musical_context, get_transport_recording_context and get_set_mixer. Resolve the intended Set, explicit musical range and new absolute .wav destination in an existing directory. The tool supports English Live on macOS, Main stereo WAV at 44.1/48/88.2/96 kHz and 16/24/32-bit (32-bit float). Resolve unsupported stem, MP3, video or other delivery requests explicitly; do not substitute a short Resampling capture or silently change format. Ask one focused question when destination, materially different range or format remains unclear.
2. Export requires stopped transport, recording off, unarmed tracks and no pending launches. Resolve these conditions only within existing authority; never silently disarm unrelated tracks or stop a performance. Read current Session/Arrangement overrides, mute/solo and routing. Main export includes current Session overrides and Main processing. Returning tracks to Arrangement, changing processing or preparing a different mix needs its own scope. Export does not isolate stems or establish what reached the speakers.
3. Plan export_audio_mix with path, start and length in native bars.beats.sixteenths coordinates (start 1.1.1; four-bar length 4.0.0), requested sampleRate/bitDepth, fresh expectedStateVersion and expectedSetFingerprint. Include intended effect tails in the length. Do not assume four beats per bar or convert across meter changes without evidence. The native dialog must read back the requested coordinates exactly. Tempo automation can affect measured duration; do not infer seconds from a single tempo value. The tool requests stereo WAV with normalization, loop rendering, MP3, video and analysis files off, and no dither for integer PCM. Export preferences are updated.
4. Review the exact destination and plan warning, then confirm the unchanged plan. The updated bridge binds native document identity and observed mix controls, not every note, device parameter, automation value or hidden state. Those contents can include intervening edits. The host rechecks context and the absent destination before the Export and Save stages. It never accepts an overwrite prompt. Preserve user changes to a prepared dialog; stale or changed input requires fresh observation, not blind replay.
5. Wait for the result. artifactVerified:true verifies a regular nonempty WAV's channels, sample rate and PCM format, stable file identity and SHA-256 hash; report measured duration and contextUnchanged separately. uiComplete:true means native dialogs closed. Live provides no completion receipt through this workflow: renderCompletion remains "unconfirmed" and exportVerified remains false, because cancellation could leave a valid partial WAV. Inspect duration and content against the requested range before declaring delivery complete; do not infer expected seconds from a single tempo reading when automation or meter changes may apply. A file alone, a pressed Save button or exportRequested:true does not prove completion. Native rendering can continue after an MCP timeout or cancellation; a render exceeding the bounded wait remains unverified. On uncertainty, inspect Live and the exact destination before any retry. Preserve partial files and never delete or overwrite them to hide a failed export.
6. Inspect the exact exported file with analyze_audio_file if measurements were requested. Recheck its hash before relying on the result. Loudness, peak or waveform measurements do not establish musical quality or that the user heard the export. Keep the Set's musical material, mix controls and saved file unchanged; native export is not saving or collecting the Project. Report destination, native range/format, measured file evidence, partial/unknown outcomes and any dialog needing attention. ${confirmWorkflow}`)]})
